  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1 DESCRIPTION...\n\nAfter areas of early morning low clouds and fog we should see\nincreasing sunshine, especially on the coastal plain and across\nsouthern NH. As a result, temperature will warm into the 80s at\nmost locations. A cold front will approach during the afternoon\nhours, but the deeper forcing for ascent associated with it will\nweaken considerably by early evening. Therefore, most showers\nand any thunderstorms associated with it will probably weaken or\nfall apart on approach from the west. However, heating of the\nday may support a couple of convective showers or storms across\ncentral and northern NH late in the afternoon and early evening.\nAreas of fog are again likely later tonight, especially in the\nCT River Valley and Midcoast of Maine.\n\nThe cold front continues to sag southward into southern New\nEngland on Thursday. At this time most ensemble guidance\nindicates that it will sag far enough south so that a developing\nwave along said front remains well to our south also. This would\nkeep a possible round of showers and thunderstorms well to our\nsouth later Thursday as well. However, there are some model\noutliers that bring the wave farther north and thus a more\nshowery scenario late Thursday to our southern zones. However,\nthe forecast favors the drier majority at this time.\n\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2 DESCRIPTION...\n\nHigh pressure then builds in Friday and the first part of Saturday, \nkeeping the weather dry for much of the area. The high does move \noff to the northeast later Saturday as low pressure emerges off \nthe Mid-Atlantic coast, and moisture will start to increase from\nsouth to north over northern New England, potentially enough \nfor a few showers along the coast and southern NH during the day\nSaturday. But again, most will be dry as the better chances \nhold off until the second half of the weekend. Highs will in the\n70s to low 80s and lows mostly in the 50s, except some embedded\nupper 40s.\n\nThis initial low will keep a chance for showers along the coast and \nsouthern NH Saturday night, but rain chances further increase \nareawide Sunday into Sunday night as a 500mb approaches from \nthe west and encompasses much of the US East Coast. There's a \nstrong signal in the ensemble for most of the area to see \nmeasurable rain, although there are timing differences. If the \n500 mb trough remains an open wave the rain looks more in the \nSunday-Sunday night timeframe, but if it closes off, then a \nslower onset and end will likely be the result, making the\nhigher rain chances more in the Sunday night and Monday window.\nBy Tuesday, we should see a return to drier conditions, except \nthere may be some upslope showers in the mountains.",
